Title:
Piclang — Dual-OS x86-64 Shellcode Programming Language for Red-Team Implants

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Piclang is an experimental, statically typed language (C/Go-inspired) that compiles directly into raw position-independent x86-64 shellcode intended for implant development. A single compiled payload is designed to run on both Windows and Linux by detecting the OS at runtime and dynamically resolving required APIs (Windows DLL exports via PEB walking; Linux libc/ELF symbols via link_map/r_debug discovery). It includes a purpose-built standard library for shellcode contexts (heap bootstrap, strings, files, process execution, networking, crypto, hashing) and a harness to run generated shellcode. The compiler applies default obfuscation such as string obfuscation and randomized section names. This is useful for red teamers and offensive tool developers who want portable PIC shellcode without hand-rolling low-level loaders and resolvers. It’s interesting because it operationalizes “write once, run on Windows+Linux” shellcode with a higher-level developer experience while keeping a shellcode-native runtime model.

Technical Focus:
- Position-independent x86-64 shellcode compilation pipeline
- Cross-platform runtime bootstrap (Windows/Linux OS detection, heap wiring)
- Dynamic API resolution (Windows PEB module/export hashing; Linux link_map/r_debug symbol lookup)
- Shellcode-oriented standard library (process, file I/O, net/HTTP, time, env, path)
- Built-in obfuscation (string obfuscation, section name randomization)
- Minimal crypto/hash primitives for implant functionality (RC4, XOR, Base64, MD5/SHA*)

Use Cases:
- Build portable Windows+Linux shellcode implants from one codebase
- Rapid prototyping of BOF/shellcode capabilities (file ops, process spawn, HTTP beacons)
- Developing custom loaders/stagers that avoid static imports by runtime resolving APIs
- Creating obfuscated shellcode payloads with reduced manual PIC engineering
- Researching/demonstrating cross-OS shellcode bootstrapping and symbol resolution techniques

Keywords:
Piclang, shellcode, position-independent code, x86-64, red team implant, compiler, BOF, C2, Windows PEB walk, export hashing, djb2 hash, Linux link_map, r_debug, ELF symbol resolution, dynamic API resolution, string obfuscation, section randomization, RC4, Base64, HTTP client